#!/bin/sh
# lvi-search [-w auto|WID] [-i] [-b] [--word] [--last] [PATTERN]
# lvi-search --motion [-i] [-b] [--word] [--last] TMPFILE COUNT LINE COL ARG
#
# Search a running lvi from outside it, in two shapes. As a LIST producer it
# greps the *live* buffer over the control socket, builds the `search` list,
# focuses it, and jumps to the first match past the cursor -- `n`/`N` then step
# it like any other list, so search is a degenerate quickfix. As a `:motion`
# FILTER (`--motion`) it answers with one target instead, which is the half a
# list cannot be: an operator needs its range before the command finishes, and a
# list arrives over the socket afterwards, so `d/foo` is not something the list
# shape can do.
#
# Because it reads the buffer via `%p` it searches UNSAVED content, and the line
# numbers map straight back. PATTERN is a POSIX BRE, vi's own dialect.
#
# BOTH SHAPES, SAME KEYS. A `map` and a `motion` on one key do not collide: lvi
# expands maps only for the FIRST key of a command (normal.lua's `first_key`), so
# a bare press takes the map and an operator-pending press takes the motion.
# `/` gives you every hit to walk; `d/` deletes to the next one. Bind both:
#     map / :prompt / bg lvi-search -- "$LVI_INPUT"<CR>       " walk the list
#     map ? :prompt ? bg lvi-search -b -- "$LVI_INPUT"<CR>    " ...backward
#     map * :bg lvi-search --word<CR>           " the word under the cursor
#     map # :bg lvi-search --word -b<CR>        " ...backward
#     map n :bg lvi-list next<CR>               " step the focused list
#     map N :bg lvi-list prev<CR>
#     motion / prompt lvi-search --motion       " d/foo, c/foo, y/foo
#     motion ? prompt lvi-search --motion -b    " d?foo
#     motion * lvi-search --motion --word       " d*
#     motion # lvi-search --motion --word -b
# From another terminal, pass a WID: `lvi-search -w auto foo`.
#
# `n`/`N` are deliberately NOT motions here. Bare `n` steps whichever list is
# focused -- lint, spell, git hunks -- so a `dn` that meant "to the next search
# match" would not mirror it. To delete back to a point you reached by stepping
# a list, use the previous-context mark instead -- `d` then two backticks. Every
# list step is jump-class, so the mark is already where you left. Add
# `motion n lvi-search --motion --last` if you
# want it anyway; it is well defined (the next match of the last pattern), just
# not the same place `n` goes.
#
# WHY ONE SCRIPT. The two shapes share the pattern, the case policy, the BRE
# dialect and the matcher. Split across two programs, "the last pattern" was a
# contract two stores had to keep in step by hand, and `/foo` followed by `d/`
# could disagree. One program owns it, so they cannot.
#
# WHO PROMPTS. The editor does, on both shapes: `:prompt` for the map, the
# `prompt` keyword for the motion. It has to, because the only terminal we could
# read a key from is the one `:!` hands over -- in cooked mode, on the primary
# screen, with the tty's line editor deciding what the keys mean and its echo
# landing on a screen the editor does not repaint. Prompting in the editor also
# leaves us with nothing interactive to do, which is what lets the map run us
# under `:bg`, with no terminal handover. The `read` below is for a hand
# invocation from a shell, where a pattern is missing and there is a real prompt
# to ask at.
#
# TWO INVOCATION STYLES, TWO SPAWN DISCIPLINES -- the one thing to know before
# editing. The list half is a self-backgrounding socket client: reading the
# buffer from a `:silent !` child would deadlock, since lvi's loop is frozen
# waiting on that child, so it double-forks a worker and returns at once. The
# motion half must do neither. The editor blocks on our stdout, so backgrounding
# loses the answer, and a socket call deadlocks for the same reason in reverse:
# nothing is servicing the socket while we run. `--motion` therefore has to be
# the FIRST argument, and it switches the whole program -- argv shape, output,
# error reporting. The two halves share only the helpers above the dispatch, all
# of which are pure.
#
# WHERE A SEARCH STARTS: at the cursor, as vi's does. The list is seeded from the
# cursor position (`lvi-list put --at-cursor`) and then stepped, so the first hop
# lands on the match AFTER the cursor rather than at the top of the file. `-b` is
# the backward search, vi's `?` and `#`: the mirror seed, then a step back. A
# search that ignored the cursor would just be a project-wide grep narrowed to
# one file, which is a different tool. The motion shape takes the COUNTth match
# strictly past the cursor for the same reason, so `/` never lands where you
# already are and `3/foo` skips two.
#
# THE MATCHER, and why it is not grep. Entries carry the match's EXTENT
# (`file:LINE.C1-C2:`), one per OCCURRENCE, so a second match further along a
# line is its own stop, the seed can resume from between two matches on one line,
# and `--paint=extent` lights the match itself. Finding that is `lvi-bre-locate`,
# the shared backend: it speaks vi's BRE and reports POSITIONS, which no single
# obvious tool does (grep will not say where a match sits, awk speaks ERE), by
# marking each match with sed and counting the offsets. It hands back
# LINE<TAB>COL<TAB>LEN per occurrence and we shape those into entries or into one
# target; see its header for the mechanism, the case-folding fallback and the
# exit codes.
#
# Two limits on the list side, both loud rather than silent: a zero-width match
# (`o*`, `^`) has no extent, so it lands as a bare `LINE.COL` entry; and a marker
# byte in the pattern or in the buffer -- a binary file, in practice -- falls back
# to `grep -n`, whose per-line entries are what this script emitted before. The
# motion side has nowhere to put either, so a zero-width match is skipped and a
# marker byte is an error. (See contrib/lvi-list for the entry format and who
# reads a column.)
#
# THREE DEVIATIONS FROM VI, all from the list being a walk rather than a motion
# the editor repeats. They apply to the LIST shape only; the motion shape is vi's
# on all three:
#   * No WRAPSCAN. A list is a walk with two ends, not a ring, so `n` stops on
#     the last match -- the status segment says `(end)` -- instead of resuming
#     from the top. `lvi-list first`/`last` are the wrap, done by hand. The
#     motion wraps, because a motion that stops dead at the end of the buffer is
#     not the motion vi has.
#   * `n` does not inherit the DIRECTION of the last search. n/N are plain
#     `lvi-list next`/`prev` and a list remembers only its index, not which way
#     it was seeded, so after a `?` it is `N` that keeps going backward.
#   * `n` walks a SNAPSHOT. The list is the buffer as it was when you searched;
#     the motion re-runs the matcher against the buffer as it is now, so nothing
#     goes stale as you type.
#
# THE LAST PATTERN is ours, in `$LVI_SOCK.search-pat`, written by both shapes so
# they always agree. It records how the search MATCHED and not just what it
# matched -- whole-word-ness and the resolved case folding travel with the
# pattern -- so `--last` repeats the search exactly as it was made, whatever the
# case policy has done since. An empty ARG reuses it too, so a bare `/<CR>`
# repeats the last search the way vi's does. The list shape writes it in the
# FOREGROUND, before the worker forks, so a `d/` pressed immediately after cannot
# race it. A search driven from another terminal (`-w`) has no $LVI_SOCK of its
# own and so leaves the store alone.
#
# Case: -i forces case-insensitive; LVI_SEARCH_CASE picks the default policy --
# `sensitive` (default), `ignore`, or `smart` (the vi/vim rule: insensitive unless
# PATTERN has an uppercase letter). Set it per binding to taste, e.g.
#     map / :silent !LVI_SEARCH_CASE=smart lvi-search<CR>
# How the folding is done (sed's `I` flag where there is one, `tr` where there is
# not) is lvi-bre-locate's business, not ours.
#
# A BARE `/<CR>` (an empty pattern, however it reaches us) repeats the last
# search, as vi's does -- the same reading `--last` and the motion shape give an
# empty argument. It stays silent when there is no last search: `/` then Enter is
# usually a change of mind, not a request for an error message.
#
# `--word` keeps only whole-word matches (grep's -w) and is what makes `*`
# behave. Where the word comes from differs by shape, because the `$LVI_*` env is
# refreshed only for ordinary spawns and a `:motion` filter is not one: the list
# shape reads `$LVI_CWORD`, the motion shape reads it off TMPFILE itself. The
# boundary test is the backend's.
#
# Config: LVI (client, default `lvi`), LVI_SEARCH_CASE (sensitive|ignore|smart),
# LVI_SEARCH_DEBUG (capture worker stderr). Needs lvi-bre-locate on PATH, and
# lvi-list for the list shape (the motion shape needs neither it nor the socket).

# -h/--help: print this script's header comment (its full reference), then exit.
case ${1:-} in -h|--help) exec awk 'NR==1&&/^#!/{next} /^#/{sub(/^# ?/,"");print;next} {exit}' "$0" ;; esac
LVI=${LVI:-lvi}

worker= want_wid= icase= back= word= last= pat= fromstore=

# ---- the shared pattern store ------------------------------------------------
# Three fields: whole-word-ness, the RESOLVED case folding, then the pattern
# (last, so it may hold anything). Storing the resolved folding rather than
# re-deciding at repeat time is what makes a repeat exact -- the policy can be
# per-binding, and `--last` must not pick up a different one.
store=${LVI_SOCK:+$LVI_SOCK.search-pat}

store_put() {                                   # word icase pattern
  [ -n "$store" ] || return 0
  printf '%s\n%s\n%s\n' "$1" "$2" "$3" > "$store" 2>/dev/null || :
}
store_get() {                                   # sets word/icase/pat; 1 if none
  [ -n "$store" ] && [ -f "$store" ] || return 1
  word=$(sed -n 1p "$store")
  icase=$(sed -n 2p "$store")
  pat=$(sed -n '3,$p' "$store")
  [ -n "$pat" ] && fromstore=1
}
# An explicit -i wins; otherwise the policy decides. Never called on a pattern
# that came from the store -- that one carries its own answer.
resolve_case() {
  [ -n "$icase" ] && return 0
  case "${LVI_SEARCH_CASE:-sensitive}" in
    ignore) icase=1 ;;
    smart)  case "$1" in *[[:upper:]]*) ;; *) icase=1 ;; esac ;;
  esac
}

# ---- the motion shape --------------------------------------------------------
# First, and self-contained: no socket, no background, one line on stdout. See
# TWO INVOCATION STYLES. The editor appends five positionals, so flag parsing
# stops when five remain -- an ARG that looks like a flag is still an ARG.
if [ "${1:-}" = --motion ]; then
  shift
  while [ $# -gt 5 ]; do
    case $1 in
      -b|--back) back=1; shift ;;
      --word)    word=1; shift ;;
      --last)    last=1; shift ;;
      -i)        icase=1; shift ;;
      *)         break ;;
    esac
  done
  [ $# -eq 5 ] || { echo "err lvi-search --motion: bad arguments"; exit 0; }
  tmpf=$1 count=$2 cl=$3 cc=$4 arg=$5

  # The pattern, from one of three places. `--word` reads it off the buffer;
  # --last and an empty prompt read the store (restoring how that search
  # matched, so a repeat is exact); otherwise it is what was typed.
  if [ -n "$word" ]; then
    pat=$(LC_ALL=C awk -v l="$cl" -v c="$cc" 'NR == l {
            s = $0
            # Walk out from the cursor byte to the word edges (vi word bytes).
            if (substr(s, c, 1) !~ /[[:alnum:]_]/) exit
            a = c; while (a > 1 && substr(s, a - 1, 1) ~ /[[:alnum:]_]/) a--
            b = c; while (b < length(s) && substr(s, b + 1, 1) ~ /[[:alnum:]_]/) b++
            print substr(s, a, b - a + 1)
          }' "$tmpf")
    [ -n "$pat" ] || { echo "err no word under the cursor"; exit 0; }
    resolve_case "$pat"
  elif [ -n "$last" ] || [ -z "$arg" ]; then
    store_get || { echo "err no previous search"; exit 0; }
  else
    pat=$arg
    resolve_case "$pat"
  fi

  hits=$(lvi-bre-locate ${icase:+-i} ${word:+--word} -- "$pat" "$tmpf" 2>/dev/null)
  case $? in
    0) ;;
    2|4) echo "err bad pattern: $pat"; exit 0 ;;
    3) echo "err buffer holds a marker byte"; exit 0 ;;
    *) echo "err lvi-search: lvi-bre-locate failed (is it on PATH?)"; exit 0 ;;
  esac

  # Remember it, unless we just read it back. Rewriting on every `n` would churn
  # the file to say what it already says.
  [ -z "$fromstore" ] && store_put "$word" "$icase" "$pat"

  printf '%s\n' "$hits" | LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' -v back="$back" -v n="$count" \
                                       -v cl="$cl" -v cc="$cc" '
    $3 > 0 { k++; L[k] = $1; C[k] = $2 }          # zero-width: nowhere to land
    END {
      if (!k) { print "err pattern not found"; exit }
      # The COUNTth match past the cursor, wrapping at the end -- vi wrapscan.
      # The list is in buffer order, so forward walks it up from the first match
      # past the cursor and backward walks it down from the last one before.
      step = back ? -1 : 1
      start = 0
      if (!back) { for (i = 1; i <= k; i++) if (L[i] > cl || (L[i] == cl && C[i] > cc)) { start = i; break }
                   if (!start) start = 1 }                     # everything behind: wrap
      else       { for (i = k; i >= 1; i--) if (L[i] < cl || (L[i] == cl && C[i] < cc)) { start = i; break }
                   if (!start) start = k }                     # everything ahead: wrap
      idx = start + (n - 1) * step
      # Wrap the count too, so 5/foo in a three-match buffer keeps going round.
      idx = ((idx - 1) % k + k) % k + 1
      printf "char %d %d\n", L[idx], C[idx]
    }'
  exit 0
fi

# ---- the list shape ----------------------------------------------------------
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case $1 in
    --worker) worker=1; shift ;;
    -w) want_wid=${2:-}; [ -n "$want_wid" ] || { echo "lvi-search: -w needs auto|WID" >&2; exit 2; }; shift 2 ;;
    -i) icase=1; shift ;;
    -b) back=1; shift ;;
    --word) word=1; shift ;;
    --last) last=1; shift ;;
    --) pat=${2:-}; break ;;          # end of flags: what follows is the pattern
    *)  pat=$1; break ;;
  esac
done
target=${want_wid:-${LVI_WID:-auto}}
wflag=; [ -n "$want_wid" ] && wflag="-w $want_wid"
# One flag, three spellings: the prompt character, the seed, and the step.
dir=/; seed=--at-cursor;      step=next
[ -n "$back" ] && { dir='?'; seed=--at-cursor-back; step=prev; }

# A notice with no tty to print on and a loop that may be frozen: background it,
# so this is safe from a `:silent !` child as well as a `:bg` one.
notify() { ( "$LVI" -w "$target" --detach -- "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1 & ) ; }

# Resolve the pattern in the FOREGROUND -- this is where the tty is (`:!` handed
# it to us) and where the store write has to happen, before the worker forks.
if [ -z "$worker" ]; then
  if [ -n "$word" ]; then
    pat=${LVI_CWORD:-}
    [ -n "$pat" ] || { notify "msge no word under the cursor"; exit 0; }
    resolve_case "$pat"
  elif [ -n "$last" ]; then
    store_get || { notify "msge no previous search"; exit 0; }
  else
    # A pattern typed at a shell (or under the older `:silent !` binding, where
    # `:!` hands over the terminal). The test is stderr, NOT stdin: `:bg`
    # redirects only the output, so the editor's raw-mode tty is still on stdin
    # and a `read` here would eat the keys being typed AT the editor. With no
    # terminal to ask on there is nothing to ask -- the map's `:prompt` already
    # did, and its answer is what we were handed.
    if [ -z "$pat" ] && [ -t 2 ] && [ -t 0 ]; then
      printf '%s' "$dir" >&2                      # prompt with the key you pressed
      IFS= read -r pat || exit 0
    fi
    if [ -z "$pat" ]; then
      store_get || exit 0                         # bare `/<CR>`: vi repeats the
    else                                          # last search, and so do we
      resolve_case "$pat"
    fi
  fi
  # Before the fork: a `d/` pressed the instant this returns must not race the
  # worker for the store (see THE LAST PATTERN).
  [ -z "$fromstore" ] && store_put "$word" "$icase" "$pat"

  # Detach the buffer-reading half; return at once so lvi's loop resumes and can
  # service the worker's socket traffic. The case folding is passed resolved, so
  # the worker never has to decide it a second time.
  ( "$0" --worker $wflag ${icase:+-i} ${back:+-b} ${word:+--word} -- "$pat" \
      >"${LVI_SEARCH_DEBUG:-/dev/null}" 2>&1 & )
  exit 0
fi

# --- worker ------------------------------------------------------------------
# The buffer's filename (entries are file:line:text so cross-file tools share
# the format). `path` is empty for a pathless buffer -- unsaved, stdin, scratch
# -- so those entries carry no file and lvi-list jumps stay put. Safe because
# lvi-search only ever matches THIS buffer, so a match never needs a
# cross-file :e in the first place.
file=${LVI_FILE:-$("$LVI" -w "$target" -- path)}

# Validate the pattern BEFORE reading the buffer -- `PATTERN /dev/null` runs
# lvi-bre-locate's checks against no input at all -- so a typo costs nothing and
# is reported where there is still someone to tell.
lvi-bre-locate ${icase:+-i} -- "$pat" /dev/null 2>/dev/null
case $? in
  2) "$LVI" -w "$target" --detach -- "hl search" "hl search-cur" "status search" \
                                     "msge bad pattern $dir$pat$dir"
     exit 0 ;;
esac

# The buffer goes to a file, not a variable: lvi-bre-locate reads it twice (the
# original lines and the marked stream) and so do we, for an entry's text. Beside
# the socket when we know where that is, since the file holds buffer content.
tdir=${LVI_SOCK%/*}; [ -n "$tdir" ] && [ -d "$tdir" ] || tdir=${TMPDIR:-/tmp}
buf=$(mktemp "$tdir/lvi-search.XXXXXX") || exit 1
hits=$(mktemp "$tdir/lvi-search.hits.XXXXXX") || exit 1
trap 'rm -f "$buf" "$hits"' EXIT HUP INT TERM
"$LVI" -w "$target" -- '%p' > "$buf"

# LINE<TAB>COL<TAB>LEN per occurrence, in vi's own BRE (see THE MATCHER).
lvi-bre-locate ${icase:+-i} ${word:+--word} -- "$pat" "$buf" > "$hits" 2>/dev/null
locate=$?
if [ "$locate" = 0 ]; then
  # A zero-width match (`o*`, `^`) has a position and no extent, so it lands as a
  # bare `LINE.COL`; anything else carries its range.
  matches=$(LC_ALL=C awk -F'\t' -v f="$file" '
    NR == FNR { o[FNR] = $0; next }                  # the original lines
    $3 > 0 { printf "%s:%d.%d-%d: %s\n", f, $1, $2, $2 + $3 - 1, o[$1]; next }
            { printf "%s:%d.%d: %s\n",   f, $1, $2, o[$1] }' "$buf" "$hits")
else
  # Marker bytes in the pattern (4) or in the buffer (3): no extents are
  # trustworthy, so fall back to `grep -n` and the per-line entries this script
  # emitted before the marker pass existed.
  matches=$(grep -n ${icase:+-i} ${word:+-w} -- "$pat" "$buf" | awk -v f="$file" '{print f":"$0}')
fi
if [ -z "$matches" ]; then
  # Clear the paint (both groups -- the current-entry mark would otherwise stay
  # on screen) and the stale counter; the notice itself is ephemeral, so :msge,
  # not a status segment. grep exit 2 is a bad BRE, not an empty result.
  # (A bad pattern never reaches here -- it is caught before the buffer is read.)
  notice="$dir$pat$dir no match"                   # echo the pattern as you typed it
  "$LVI" -w "$target" --detach -- "hl search" "hl search-cur" "status search" "msge $notice"
  exit 0
fi
# search owns its look so it shows without a theme (see CLAUDE.md). It re-sends
# this on every search, so an rc `hi search` gets overwritten -- restyle HERE
# (or in a wrapper), not in the rc.
# pri=10 lifts the mark above syntax highlighting (pri 0) so it shows through a
# styled first column (e.g. a Markdown `1.` ordered-list marker). `search-cur`
# sits one above that and adds bold: with every match lit, the one you are ON has
# to read differently from the rest, and bold says so without assuming a palette.
"$LVI" -w "$target" --detach -- "hi search reverse pri=10" "hi search-cur reverse bold pri=11"
# Seed from the cursor, then take one step off it -- that pair is the whole of
# "search starts where I am" (see WHERE A SEARCH STARTS). lvi-list owns the
# index; we name a direction and it does the arithmetic.
# --paint=extent: the match is the thing you are looking at, so light it rather
# than sign the line (see PAINT POLICY in contrib/lvi-list). Columns are bytes.
printf '%s\n' "$matches" \
  | lvi-list $wflag put search --focus --cols=byte --paint=extent "$seed"
lvi-list $wflag "$step"
